WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.176
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:08.758
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.058 --> 00:00:14.230
Unit three: Generational Gaps. Today you will show contrast with 'although' and 'even though'.

4
00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.147
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.484
Part one: words. You will learn twelve new words.

6
00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:23.734
Generation Z.

7
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:30.546
Generation Z means people born from about 1997 to 2012.

8
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:33.475
Generation Z grew up with smartphones.

9
00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:37.177
Your turn. Say: Generation Z.

10
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:41.325
Generation Z.

11
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:43.142
Baby boomer.

12
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:50.991
Baby boomer means a person born from about 1946 to 1964.

13
00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:53.536
My grandfather is a baby boomer.

14
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.744
Your turn. Say: Baby boomer.

15
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.787
Baby boomer.

16
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.050
Perspective shift.

17
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.978
Perspective shift means a change in how you see something.

18
00:01:07.583 --> 00:01:10.361
Talking with my grandma caused a perspective shift.

19
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.904
Your turn. Say: Perspective shift.

20
00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:18.093
Perspective shift.

21
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:20.397
Traditional values.

22
00:01:21.383 --> 00:01:24.823
Traditional values means old beliefs passed down in a culture.

23
00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:27.765
My parents hold traditional values.

24
00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.444
Your turn. Say: Traditional values.

25
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.719
Traditional values.

26
00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:37.624
Modern outlook.

27
00:01:38.617 --> 00:01:41.417
Modern outlook means a new way of seeing the world.

28
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:44.527
Young people often have a modern outlook.

29
00:01:45.117 --> 00:01:48.303
Your turn. Say: Modern outlook.

30
00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:52.446
Modern outlook.

31
00:01:53.183 --> 00:01:54.382
Bridge the gap.

32
00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:58.590
Bridge the gap means to bring two different groups closer.

33
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:01.264
Family dinners help bridge the gap.

34
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.932
Your turn. Say: Bridge the gap.

35
00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:08.814
Bridge the gap.

36
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:10.934
Mutual respect.

37
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.354
Mutual respect means respect that both sides give.

38
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.402
Mutual respect makes families strong.

39
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:21.773
Your turn. Say: Mutual respect.

40
00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:26.122
Mutual respect.

41
00:02:26.883 --> 00:02:28.059
Cultural shift.

42
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.582
Cultural shift means a big change in how a society thinks.

43
00:02:33.183 --> 00:02:35.392
Online shopping is a cultural shift.

44
00:02:35.983 --> 00:02:38.531
Your turn. Say: Cultural shift.

45
00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:42.844
Cultural shift.

46
00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:44.596
Adapt.

47
00:02:45.583 --> 00:02:48.639
Adapt means to change to fit a new situation.

48
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:52.004
My grandmother adapted to video calls.

49
00:02:52.617 --> 00:02:55.141
Your turn. Say: Adapt.

50
00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.094
Adapt.

51
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:00.967
Understanding.

52
00:03:01.983 --> 00:03:04.947
Understanding means knowing how others feel and think.

53
00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.561
Understanding grows when we listen.

54
00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.127
Your turn. Say: Understanding.

55
00:03:13.967 --> 00:03:15.223
Understanding.

56
00:03:15.983 --> 00:03:16.868
Dialogue.

57
00:03:17.883 --> 00:03:20.626
Dialogue means a conversation to share ideas.

58
00:03:21.217 --> 00:03:23.321
Open dialogue reduces conflict.

59
00:03:23.917 --> 00:03:26.523
Your turn. Say: Dialogue.

60
00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:30.507
Dialogue.

61
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:32.390
Common ground.

62
00:03:33.383 --> 00:03:36.300
Common ground means ideas that both sides agree on.

63
00:03:36.917 --> 00:03:40.158
We found common ground: we both love family.

64
00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.565
Your turn. Say: Common ground.

65
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:47.645
Common ground.

66
00:03:48.433 --> 00:03:51.817
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

67
00:03:52.683 --> 00:03:55.507
Visal, do you ever disagree with your grandparents?

68
00:03:56.617 --> 00:04:01.556
Sometimes. Although they hold traditional values, they listen to me.

69
00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:03.976
How do you bridge the gap?

70
00:04:05.083 --> 00:04:10.092
We talk over dinner. Even though our views differ, we find common ground.

71
00:04:11.183 --> 00:04:14.230
That's mutual respect. Has anything changed?

72
00:04:15.317 --> 00:04:22.160
Yes! Even though Grandpa is a baby boomer, he adapted to video calls.

73
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.893
Part three: grammar.

74
00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:28.063
Grammar focus: although and even though.

75
00:04:28.363 --> 00:04:29.573
Look at the pattern.

76
00:04:30.283 --> 00:04:32.875
Although views differ, mutual respect helps.

77
00:04:33.783 --> 00:04:36.340
Even though times change, values matter.

78
00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:39.296
We get along even though we are different.

79
00:04:40.233 --> 00:04:44.209
Don't use 'but' together with 'although'. 'Even though' is stronger than 'although'.

80
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.332
Be careful! Don't say: Although views differ, but we respect each other.

81
00:04:51.267 --> 00:04:54.683
Say: Although views differ, we respect each other.

82
00:04:54.983 --> 00:04:56.541
Don't use 'but' with 'although'.

83
00:04:57.333 --> 00:05:00.032
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

84
00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:01.904
Bridging the Gap.

85
00:05:02.204 --> 00:05:05.690
Generation Z and baby boomers often see the world differently.

86
00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:11.033
Bridging the generational gap requires dialogue and mutual respect.

87
00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:15.612
Although views differ, both sides can learn from each other.

88
00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:20.213
Traditional values and a modern outlook can find common ground.

89
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.848
Even though times change, love for family stays the same.

90
00:05:25.933 --> 00:05:29.854
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

91
00:05:30.717 --> 00:05:33.111
blank views differ, we respect each other.

92
00:05:33.411 --> 00:05:36.821
A, Although. B, But. C, Because.

93
00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:42.134
The answer is A: Although.

94
00:05:43.283 --> 00:05:46.038
Ideas that both sides agree on are common blank.

95
00:05:46.338 --> 00:05:49.561
A, ground. B, gap. C, shift.

96
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.914
The answer is A: ground.

97
00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:58.676
To change to fit a new situation is to blank.

98
00:05:58.976 --> 00:06:02.038
A, adapt. B, bridge. C, shift.

99
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.611
The answer is A: adapt.

100
00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.859
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

101
00:06:12.717 --> 00:06:16.353
How can teenagers and older generations understand each other better?

102
00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:26.592
Now listen to Visal.

103
00:06:26.892 --> 00:06:36.502
They can talk more. Although we grew up in different times, we can share stories. Even though teenagers love phones, we can listen to our grandparents.

104
00:06:36.802 --> 00:06:38.534
Excellent answer, Visal!

105
00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:43.554
Great work! Now you can use twelve new words and although and even though.

106
00:06:43.854 --> 00:06:48.340
Today's value is respect for elders. Listen to and help older people.

107
00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.188
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

108
00:06:51.488 --> 00:06:54.686
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

